          ]]>
        </code>

      </program>
    </project>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="challenge-dataset">
    <title>Optional challenge with a dataset</title>

    <p>
      If your class has time, you can try the following open-ended challenge
      that uses a dataset of your choice. You could work in pairs for this
      challenge. Choose a dataset from the files <url
      href="external/_static/datasets/pokemon.csv">pokemon.csv</url>, <url
      href="external/_static/datasets/WorldIndicators2000.csv">WorldIndicators2000.csv</url>,
      or <url
      href="external/_static/datasets/StateData2020-CDC-Census.csv">StateData2020-CDC-Census.csv</url>
      to read into an array of objects. The activecode window below is set up to
      use these files. Look at the columns of the dataset you have chosen at the
      bottom of this web page to decide on the name and at least 3 attributes
      for your class. Each row in the data file will be an object of your class
      that you will add to the array. If you find another data CSV file online
      that you would like to use, you can read from a URL instead of a file in
      Java using the <c>java.net</c> package following the directions here <url
      href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/networking/urls/readingURL.html">https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/networking/urls/readingURL.html</url>.
    </p>

    <p>
      After you have chosen an input file, use the Pokémon exercise in the
      section above as a guide to:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Design a class for the input file that you have chosen. Choose at
          least 3 attributes that can be found in the file for your class. Write
          a constructor that takes in these attributes as parameters and saves
          them into instance variables. You may need to add some getters and a
          <c>toString</c> method as well.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>Declare an array of your class type.</p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>Read in the data from the file.</p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Inside a loop, split each line into its attributes and create an
          object for your class using its constructor. Add the object to the
          array.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Do something interesting with the data using a loop, for example you
          could find the maximum or minimum value of an attribute or print out
          all the objects that have the same attribute value.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <project label="challenge-file">
      <statement>
